<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pastry War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1838">1838</a>–<a href="/years/1839">1839</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pastry War was the first French intervention in Mexico, in 1838–1839. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, pressing claims for damages to its nationals, including a French pastry cook in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>, blockaded Mexican ports and took the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-juan-de-ulua">San Juan de Ulúa</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz">Veracruz</a>. Santa Anna lost a leg defending the city, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> agreed under British mediation to pay 600,000 pesos.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pastry-war.jpg" alt="Pastry War" width="320" height="229" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Horace Vernet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%C3%89pisode_de_l%27exp%C3%A9dition_du_Mexique_en_1838_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In the early years of the Mexican republic, factions competed for control and there was widespread disorder. Fighting often destroyed or looted private property, and foreigners whose property was damaged by rioters or bandits were usually unable to obtain compensation from the Mexican government, so they appealed to their own governments. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> had become <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>'s third largest trade partner after the establishment of diplomatic relations, but French goods paid higher taxes because France had not secured trade agreements like those of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and the United Kingdom.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>A pastry chef known only as Monsieur Remontel complained to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-philippe-i">Louis-Philippe</a> that Mexican officers had looted his shop in Tacubaya in 1832 and demanded 60,000 pesos. Mexican sources said the officers, from Santa Anna's government, had simply refused to pay their bills. The complaint gave the war its name. It was joined by others, among them the looting of French shops at the Parian market in 1828 and the execution of a French citizen accused of piracy in 1837. In 1838 the prime minister, Louis-Mathieu Molé, demanded 600,000 pesos in damages.</p><p>When President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anastasio-bustamante">Anastasio Bustamante</a> paid nothing, the king ordered Rear Admiral Charles Baudin to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> all Mexican ports on the Gulf from Yucatán to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande">Rio Grande</a>, bombard the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-juan-de-ulua">San Juan de Ulúa</a> and seize <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz">Veracruz</a>. French forces took Veracruz by December 1838, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> declared war. Trade was cut off, and Mexicans smuggled imports through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corpus-christi-feast">Corpus Christi</a> in the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>, whose forces patrolled the bay to stop them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-lopez-de-santa-anna">Antonio López de Santa Anna</a> left retirement, surveyed the defences of Veracruz and led Mexican forces at the Battle of Veracruz. A skirmish with the French rear guard left him wounded in the leg, which was amputated and buried with full military honours.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The French withdrew on 9 March 1839 after a British-brokered peace treaty. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> agreed to pay 600,000 pesos in damages to French citizens, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> received promises of future trade commitments in place of war indemnities. The damages were never paid, and this was later used as one justification for the second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-french-intervention-in-mexico">French intervention in Mexico</a> of 1861. After the Mexican victory in 1867 and the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-french-empire">Second French Empire</a> in 1870, the two countries did not resume diplomatic relations until 1880, when both renounced claims relating to the wars.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pastry War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
